commenti.body:
It is interesting that you read (or see) a possible reference to annatta within the film. The film does support such a tentative reading (the real Gary was a Buddhist, assuming 'Gary' could have ever been a real or fixed entity)

&#x200B;

My take, though, is that Hit Man is less a mediation of 'not self' and more an exploration of self as performance (or performativity) and/or social construction. Themes that the film couldn't make more explicit and sets out to explore.

&#x200B;

 This suggests - to me anyway - that 'the self' elucidated within the film really exists or that there is an underlying and abiding self that persists and falls in love. Otherwise, 'who' is doing the performance and what is the self (or selves) constructed out of and continues to feel across contexts regardless? 

&#x200B;

So I would counter - assuming 'I' really exist and 'you' are really reading this - that the film is not so much a deep dive into anything but a swim in the shallow end. From my perspective, the film takes on a number of incompatible philosophical principles that it is not really interested in exploring (the self as illusion is not the same as the self as construct or performance) and Hit Man is merely playing dress up.

 Specifically, it isn't difficult to see why an actor wanted to (co)write and act in a partially autobiographical film  - it spoke to his  persistent desire that we can all be actors taking on different roles or can play different parts that tap into abiding aspects of our real or underlying selves.
